# M4 touch-plate LED: patch order decides who owns the plate (issue #80).
#
# On hardware the plate LED is ONE register pair per fader, so the last write of
# the cycle wins -- and the cycle runs the circuits in patch order
# (manual/basics.md §5.2). A `motoquencer` paints the plates of the faders it
# owns straight into FaderState; an `L`/`R` register write lands on the same
# state through Output::set. Whichever runs LAST is what the LED shows.
#
# Here: motoquencer first, the R write second -> the R write owns fader 1 and
# the motoquencer keeps faders 2..4. The mirrored file
# m4-plate-led-patch-order-reversed.gold proves the other direction.
tickrate 100
patch <<<
[m4]

[motoquencer]
    clock       = I1
    numsteps    = 4
    numfaders   = 4
    defaultgate = 1
    buttoncolor = 0.8
    cv          = O1
    gate        = O2

[copy]
    input  = 0.4
    output = R1.1
>>>
# Fader 1: the later [copy] overwrites the motoquencer's gate colour.
@0 expect F1.color 0.4 tol 1e-6
@0 expect F1.led 1 tol 1e-6
# Faders 2..4: untouched by the copy, still the motoquencer's buttoncolor.
@0 expect F2.color 0.8 tol 1e-6
@0 expect F3.color 0.8 tol 1e-6
@0 expect F4.color 0.8 tol 1e-6
# Every tick, not just the first.
@5 expect F1.color 0.4 tol 1e-6
@5 expect F2.color 0.8 tol 1e-6
